	The [footnotes] have spoilers.

	Pasi:  19 new Sokoban levels.  Pasi has a gift for making
good-looking levels.  Sokoban levels don't leave much play for orna-
mentation, but Pasi still managed.

	I dislike Sokoban.  Still, I found that fighting through 
Pasi's levels and taking a luck penalty or two is less boring than 
doing the old Sokoban levels using a memorized procedure.

	Pasi:  two Medusa levels.  These look very nice.  One has
scattered islands, three buildings, and ravens in trees [1].  The
other has a half-sunken island with a ruined palace, a yellow dragon
plus offspring, and some black nagas [2].  

	Pasi:  two castle levels.  I've never played these.  I looked
at the data file just now.  The first one is a castle, but won't play
at all like the old level [3].  The second one has a lava river 
stretching diagonally across the map.  The upper left corner has a 
fortress.  I have no idea how it would play.  

	Pasi:  four bigrooms.  I haven't seen or played these.  
[Pause.]  Two look like more of the usual, one has some iron bars
across the middle (which shouldn't affect play much), and one has
irregular streams of lava and water across them, which should slow
down the monsters.

	Kelly:  two minetowns.  One is very small and half-destroyed,
with encouraging engravings like "DANGER!" scattered around [4].
The other is a normal town, but with a moat.  It looks quite nice.

	Kelly:  two mine ends.  "Boulder Bonanza" has lots of boulder
traps and falling rock traps.  "The Gnome King's Apiary" has lots of
dead adventurers [5].  I nicknamed it "the Shotgun", and I left it
out of the New Toys collection, even though I like it myself.

	Rebecca (7), Naomi (8), and Menna (7) Rempt:  "Lego town".
Outside the town, there's a river and a bit of a lake.  There's a
gravel causeway across the lake to a random area with the stairs
down.  The door to the scroll shop is stuck behind the lake, which
presents a minor problem.  There are engravings like "Ga heen,
trollengebroed!" here and there, and most of the town watch is 
hiding in the (locked) temple [6].

	Me:  two mine towns.  One is the Orcus level, only with all
the shops intact.  Odd:  instead of gnomes and dwarves, there are 
just gnomish corpses.  And who let the quasit and imps in?  [7] 

	The second, "Conquered Town", has dead shopkeepers, dead
watchmen, and a *lot* of soldiers.  After the guard house, they are
peaceful:  you're here, so you're obviously one of the boys, right?  
[8]  The guys at the armories are willing to part with equipment for
a suitable payment.  

	Me:  oracle level.  I put the Oracle on an island.  The
island is swarming with nymphs, some peaceful, some not.  (I used a
trick to keep this from being as nasty as it sounds.)  [9]

	Tina Hall:  Valley of the Dead.  This one is more like a
valley than the old one.

	Kevin Wayne:  bigroom.  This is a dark Mines level.  The
monster distribution is skewed:  three monster types are selected
from nFNOPCVY@, and you get roughly half a dozen of each.

	Me:  revamp Sokoban so that it makes sense.  I showed that 
it was a testing and training area for mid-ranked minions of the 
WoY.  The wands and rings got put into shops.  Some people objected 
to a lot of the minor changes, so I would likely redo the revamp if
I update New Toys to 3.4.3.


	Me:  nine new special levels; you usually get one at about
dungeon level 5.  They average the same loot and difficulty as normal
levels.  They mainly just add flavor.

   o  "Tele traps":  a 3x4 grid of square rooms with corridors around
      them.  You use teleport traps instead of doors.  There's an
      armor shop and a vault [10].

   o  "Holy Sanctuary":  a coaligned altar + priest.  

   o  "Mini-bigroom":  a weak version of the bigroom.

   o  "Half-Assed Castle":  this is roughly based on the real Castle
      level, but it was built by someone with a shoestring budget.
      The moat monsters are two baby crocodiles, and the four dragons
      have been replaced by two lizards [11].

   o  "Foretaste of Gehennom":  cold, dark, pools of water to fall
      into, peaceful ghosts, and a miserable time finding the stairs
      down.  An engraving says:  "This foretaste of Gehennom was 
      brought to you by Orcus.  Enjoy!"  Bastard.  Even I dislike this
      level, and I designed it [12].

   o  "The Wizard's Park":  an amusement park for employees of the
      WoY.  If you're short on food, head for the refreshment stand.
      Bruce the Shark is peaceful.  (Honest.)  [13]

   o  "Broken Maze":  a Gehennom-style maze, only with so many holes
      in the walls that they don't really get in your way.

   o  "Citadel":  a very small fortress, recently taken over by 
      orcs & goblins.  The rest of the level looks a bit exotic,
      but there's really nothing of interest.

   o  "Peaceful Suburb":  a shop, some houses, peaceful kobolds,
      some children, and a pet or two.

